    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the number of rows in the display, let's analyze the structure step-by-step starting from the top row:\n\n1. The top row has 1 can.\n2. The second row has 1 + 2 = 3 cans.\n3. The third row has 3 + 2 = 5 cans.\n4. The fourth row has 5 + 2 = 7 cans.\n5. And so on...\n\nThis pattern is an arithmetic sequence where each term increases by 2. We can define the nth term of this sequence as: $a_n = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2$.\n\nThe total number of cans in the display is the sum of this arithmetic sequence up to $n$ rows.\n\nThe formula for the sum $S_n$ of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence is:\n\n$$ S_n = \\frac{n}{2}(2a + (n - 1)d) $$\n\nwhere $a$ is the first term ($a=1$ in this case), $d$ is the common difference ($d=2$).\n\nGiven that there are 100 cans in total, we have:\n\n$$ 100 = \\frac{n}{2}(2 \\cdot 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2) $$\n$$ 100 = n(n - 1) $$\n\nSolving the quadratic equation:\n\n$$(n - 10)(n + 1) = 0 $$\n\nThe roots are $n_1 = -1 \\, (rejected)$ and $n_2 = 10$. Hence, the display contains $\\boxed{10}$ rows.",
