    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we can set up an equation that represents the total number of cans. Let n be the number of rows in the display. The number of cans in each row can be represented as an arithmetic sequence: 1, 3, 5, 7, ..., (2n-1).\n\nWe can set up the equation:\n\n1 + 3 + 5 + ... + (2n-1) = 100\n\nWe know that the sum of an arithmetic sequence can be calculated using the formula:\n\nSum = (n/2)(a1 + an)\n\nwhere n is the number of terms, a1 is the first term, and an is the last term.\n\nIn this case, a1 = 1 and an = 2n-1. So, the equation becomes:\n\nSum = (n/2)(1 + (2n-1))\n\nNow, we can solve for n:\n\n(n/2)(1 + 2n-1) = 100\n(n/2)(2n) = 100\n(n^2) = 100\nn = sqrt(100)\nn = 10\n\nTherefore, there are 10 rows in the display.",
